By definition, the Bayes factor is the ratio of marginal likelihoods under the alternative and null hypotheses:
(4)
BF=P(D∣H1)P(D∣H0). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{BF}=\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(D \mid H_{1}\right)}{\mathbb{P}\left(D \mid H_{0}\right)} . \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To compute posterior probabilities, one must specify prior odds ℙ(H1)/ℙ(H0). The posterior odds are then BF × Prior odds, yielding
(5)
P(H0∣D)=11+BF×Prior odds=11+P(D∣H1)m′|H′|τ⋅P(H1)1N/′IT. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{P}\left(H_{0} \mid D\right)=\frac{1}{1+\mathrm{BF} \times \text { Prior odds }}=\frac{1}{1+\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(D \mid H_{1}\right)}{m^{\prime}\left|H^{\prime}\right| \tau} \cdot \frac{\mathbb{P}\left(H_{1}\right)}{\frac{1}{N / \prime I T}}} . \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Comparing (2), (3), and (5) suggests an approximate relationship:
(6)
BF≈ Power p. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{BF} \approx \frac{\text { Power }}{p} . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

To see the connection, consider the empirical estimates of the marginal and null cumulative distribution functions at p(k):
(11)
F^(p(k))=km, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{F}\left(p_{(k)}\right)=\frac{k}{m}, \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
F^0(p(k))=mp(k)m=p(k)(assuming p-values are uniform under the null). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{F}_{0}\left(p_{(k)}\right)=\frac{m p_{(k)}}{m}=p_{(k)} \quad$$\end{document}
The BH procedure can be derived as rejecting when the estimated FDR falls below α:
(13)
π^0F^0(p(k))F^(p(k))<α, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\hat{\pi}_{0} \hat{F}_{0}\left(p_{(k)}\right)}{\hat{F}\left(p_{(k)}\right)}<\alpha, \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where π^0\begin{document}$\hat{\pi}_{0}$\end{document} is an estimate of the proportion of true nulls. The original BH procedure implicitly takes π^0=1\begin{document}$\hat{\pi}_{0}=1$\end{document} (a conservative choice), yielding p(k) < αk/m as the rejection criterion. More sophisticated procedures estimate π0 from the data to increase power.
